# The repository root's documentation OVERLAY, managed by `cat-harness`.
#
# Declared in `cat-harness/harness.json` as `root-docs`, `scope: "repository"` —
# the same mechanism by which that instance already declares `beans/`, `todos/`,
# `memory/` and a dozen sibling instances' subdirectories at this level.
#
# Owner's ruling, 2026-09-21: "harness can have docs/ which then get listed
# under cat-harness/docs/<harness>, there is also docs/ dir in repo root managed
# by cat-harness. other harness augment or overlay ontop of that (content and
# behviors)".
#
# ## What goes here, and what does not
#
# This is the OVERLAY, not the documentation. The platform's pages live in
# `cat-harness/docs/` and STAY there — the compose ruling means they are not
# moved. A file placed here SHADOWS the page at the same path in the base
# layer; a file at a path the base layer does not have is simply added.
#
#   - a page this repository says differently from the platform's -> here, at
#     the same relative path as the one it replaces;
#   - a page about the platform itself -> `cat-harness/docs/`, where it is;
#   - another instance's own documentation -> that instance's `docs/`, listed
#     under `cat-harness/docs/<harness>/` by `mount-instance-docs.ts`.
#
# ## Why this is a `.gitkeep` and not a README
#
# Because a `README.md` here would itself be composed into the published site,
# and the property that makes wiring this into the LIVE publish path safe is
# that an empty overlay produces a BYTE-IDENTICAL site. A dotfile is ignored by
# Jekyll, so the proof stays clean. The same reason `todos/feedback/.gitkeep`
# and `beans/workflows/.gitkeep` carry their prose this way.
#
# ## Why an empty declared directory is not `dh4f` here
#
# A declared directory nothing reads is this repository's most-paid-for defect.
# This one IS read: the site build composes it over `cat-harness/docs/`. What it
# contributes today is nothing VERIFIABLY — pinned by a test — rather than
# nothing unnoticed.
